# November 23, 2026: the lower Hudson river towns of Westchester County, New York: Sleepy Hollow, Tarrytown,
# Pocantico Hills, Irvington, Dobbs Ferry, Hastings-on-Hudson, Elmsford and Yonkers. Drawn at random by
# tools/choose.py --state ny (region midh; it offered Sleepy Hollow, Mohonk Lake and Tarrytown, and start.py found
# no Atlas place within 9 km of Tarrytown).
# Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia as raw text (and the two Westchester County Register lists), Historic
# Hudson Valley's site pages, Lyndhurst's visit page, New York State Parks (Rockefeller State Park Preserve and
# Philipse Manor Hall), Westchester County Parks, the Village of Sleepy Hollow, the Reformed Church of the
# Tarrytowns, the First Baptist Church of Tarrytown, the Friends of the Old Croton Aqueduct, the Hastings Historical
# Society, the Newington-Cropsey Foundation and Untermyer Gardens Conservancy.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'midh_luaq','label':'Sleepy Hollow, Tarrytown and the river towns to Yonkers','added':'2026-11-23 00:42'}
HHV=lambda t,u: L('Historic Hudson Valley: '+t,u)
PLACES=[
N('old-dutch-church-sleepy-hollow','Old Dutch Church of Sleepy Hollow and its Burying Ground','Sleepy Hollow','midh','worship','standing',41.09028,-73.86167,'site','open','drive','cemeteries','1685–1697',
  'A small fieldstone church built for the tenants of Philipsburg Manor by Frederick Philipse, its first lord, who designed and paid for it and is said to have made the pulpit himself. The date on its bell, cast in the Netherlands, is 1685, and the congregation was organised in 1697; the church went up between those years. It is the oldest church standing in New York State. Washington Irving made the church and its churchyard the setting of The Legend of Sleepy Hollow (1820). It was repaired after a fire in 1837, restored to its old look about sixty years later and stabilised in 1960, and became a National Historic Landmark in 1961.',
  'Fieldstone walls two feet thick under a flared gambrel roof sided in clapboard, with an open octagonal wooden belfry holding the original bell. Inside are wooden pews facing a raised, ornate pulpit reached by a short spiral stair. The Old Dutch Burying Ground round it is separate from Sleepy Hollow Cemetery behind it. A few of the church\'s old yellow bricks can be seen under the southwest window, where a door once was (Wikipedia).',
  'New York\'s oldest standing church, and the churchyard of the Headless Horseman.',
  'Owned by the Reformed Church of the Tarrytowns, on the east side of US 9 (North Broadway) opposite Devries Road. The Burying Ground is open daily until 4:30 p.m.; Sunday worship is held in the church at 10:30 a.m. from June 30 through Labor Day, and visitors may be able to look round and speak with a docent after the service. Organised tour groups must ask the church first, and tour buses may not use the parking lot without Sleepy Hollow Cemetery\'s leave (the church\'s Old Dutch Church page, read November 23, 2026).',
  [W('Old Dutch Church of Sleepy Hollow'),L('Reformed Church of the Tarrytowns: Old Dutch Church','https://reformedchurchtarrytowns.org/old-dutch-church/')],
  brick='no',hours='Burying Ground daily until 4:30 p.m.; services Sundays 10:30 a.m., June 30 to Labor Day'),
N('philipsburg-manor-sleepy-hollow','Philipsburg Manor (Upper Mills)','Sleepy Hollow','midh','industry','standing',41.08853,-73.86361,'site','open','drive','','1693 on; restored to its 1750 look 1959–1969',
  'Frederick Philipse was granted Philipsburg Manor, 52,000 acres along the Hudson, by royal charter in 1693, and built a stone manor house, a mill and a wharf where the Pocantico River meets the Hudson as a depot for his Atlantic shipping. The Philipses were among the largest slaveholders in the colonial North, and the people they enslaved worked the mill, the dairy and the farm here; they are named in the 1750 inventory. The site opened to the public in 1943, passed to Sleepy Hollow Restorations (now Historic Hudson Valley) in 1951, and was closed from 1959 to 1969 for a restoration to its look in 1750. Digs in the 1940s and 1950s found the foundations of the house, mill, wharf, outbuildings and dam.',
  'The stone manor house with its cellar dairy, a working water-powered gristmill on the old foundations and a millpond crossed by a bridge, and a barn. Costumed guides tell the history of the enslaved community that ran the place. Historic Hudson Valley says the mill is entered on the tour; how much of the mill fabric is original was not checked.',
  'A colonial mill and trading post told from the side of the enslaved people who ran it.',
  'Historic Hudson Valley, 381 North Broadway (US 9); overflow lot at 100 Continental Street. Timed tickets, cheaper bought ahead: adults $18 in 2026. Guided tours take about 1 hour 45 minutes, indoors and out, with stairs (Historic Hudson Valley\'s Philipsburg Manor page, read November 23, 2026).',
  [W('Philipsburg Manor House'),HHV('Philipsburg Manor','https://hudsonvalley.org/historic-sites/philipsburg-manor/')],
  brick='?',hours='Friday to Sunday May 8 to Sep 27 and Nov 1 to 29; Mon, Wed, Thu in October; Saturday and Sunday Dec 5 to 20 (2026)'),
N('sleepy-hollow-cemetery','Sleepy Hollow Cemetery','Sleepy Hollow','midh','burial','standing',41.09662,-73.86142,'area','public','walk','cemeteries','1849',
  'A rural cemetery of about 90 acres started by a group of local men led by Washington Irving after the Rural Cemetery Act of 1847, when the Old Dutch Burying Ground was running out of room. It was incorporated as Tarrytown Cemetery in 1849 and took the name Sleepy Hollow Cemetery in 1865. Irving himself is buried here, among many well-known names; the private Rockefeller family cemetery lies behind a high fence at its north end. It was listed on the National Register in 2009.',
  'Winding drives and hillside plots behind the Old Dutch Church, with Irving\'s grave and many large family monuments. The pin is the middle of the grounds; the Old Dutch Burying Ground at its southwest corner is a separate place.',
  'Where Washington Irving lies, in the hollow he made famous.',
  'A non-profit, non-sectarian cemetery on North Broadway (US 9), Sleepy Hollow (Wikipedia). Its own website could not be opened when this was written, so its gate hours and tour times were not checked. Tour buses need the cemetery\'s leave to use the lot by the Old Dutch Church (the Reformed Church of the Tarrytowns\' page).',
  [W('Sleepy Hollow Cemetery'),L('Reformed Church of the Tarrytowns: Old Dutch Church','https://reformedchurchtarrytowns.org/old-dutch-church/')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('tarrytown-light-sleepy-hollow','Tarrytown Light (Sleepy Hollow Light)','Sleepy Hollow','midh','light','standing',41.08406,-73.87425,'exact','open','walk','','1883–1961',
  'A cast-iron "sparkplug" lighthouse on a caisson, lit in 1883 after steamship companies asked for help past the shoals off Tarrytown; it was built offshore because land on the bank was too dear. Its keepers lived inside the tower, fourteen of them in its working life, some with their families; it is the only lighthouse in Westchester County. Once the Tappan Zee Bridge was built the light was no longer needed, and it was taken out of service in 1961. The river has since been filled in round it, so it now stands just off the bank.',
  'The white conical tower at the south end of Kingsland Point Park, reached by a footbridge of about 100 feet over a riprap breakwater. Curator-led tours go inside; service animals may go to the first floor only.',
  'A keepers\' home inside a sparkplug light, now a few steps off the shore.',
  'Owned by Westchester County and part of Kingsland Point Park, which the Village of Sleepy Hollow runs. Park entrance and parking $10 for non-residents without a county park pass, $5 for residents and pass holders (Village of Sleepy Hollow page). Tours are free, by registration on Eventbrite when offered (Westchester County Parks page, read November 23, 2026); the village says they run in summer.',
  [W('Tarrytown Light'),L('Westchester County Parks: Tarrytown Lighthouse in Sleepy Hollow','https://parks.westchestercountyny.gov/tarrytown-lighthouse-in-sleepy-hollow'),
   L('Village of Sleepy Hollow: Kingsland Point Park','https://sleepyhollowny.gov/Facilities/Facility/Details/Kingsland-Point-Park-5')],
  brick='no',hours='Tours by registration when offered, mostly in summer'),
N('patriots-park-tarrytown','Patriots Park and the André Capture Monument','Tarrytown and Sleepy Hollow','midh','curio','standing',41.08167,-73.85861,'site','public','walk','','1780; monument 1853',
  'On this spot in 1780 three Patriot militiamen stopped Major John André of the British Army, travelling in civilian clothes, and found papers that exposed Benedict Arnold\'s plot. A monument went up here in 1853 on land given by William and Mary Taylor, members of the local African American community; it was one of the earliest monuments to any event of the Revolution. The ground later became part of Brookside Park, a residential development by Carrère and Hastings, then a girls\' school campus, and in the early 1900s a public park. It was listed on the National Register in 1982.',
  'A four-acre park astride André Brook, the line between the two villages, with a walkway, two stone footbridges over the creek and the monument: a pedestal topped by a bronze statue of John Paulding, one of the captors, with a bronze relief of the capture.',
  'Where Benedict Arnold\'s treason came to light.',
  'A public park on US 9 (Broadway) at the Tarrytown–Sleepy Hollow line (Wikipedia). Street parking nearby; park rules and hours not checked.',
  [W('Patriots Park (Westchester)')],
  brick='?'),
N('philipse-manor-station-sleepy-hollow','Philipse Manor station house','Sleepy Hollow','midh','rail','standing',41.09472,-73.86944,'site','view','drive','stations','1910–1911',
  'A Tudor-style station house built about 1910 and opened on January 30, 1911, for a new suburb laid out on land that had been part of Philipsburg Manor. It is one of two stations on Metro-North\'s Hudson Line on the National Register, as an intact early commuter station. After automatic ticketing replaced the agent the station house fell into disrepair; the Hudson Valley Writers Center took it over and won a Preservation League of New York State award for its work on it in 2005.',
  'The station house with arched windows, a side porch under Tudor arches and a half-timbered gable, beside the working platforms. The writers\' centre uses the inside; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Metro-North\'s Philipse Manor stop on the Hudson Line; the station house is used by the Hudson Valley Writers Center (Wikipedia). See it from the street or the platforms; trains run here, so keep off the tracks.',
  [W('Philipse Manor station')],
  brick='?'),
N('christ-episcopal-church-tarrytown','Christ Episcopal Church (Christ Church San Marcos)','Tarrytown','midh','worship','standing',41.07494,-73.85917,'exact','view','drive','','1837',
  'A red-brick Gothic Revival church built in 1837 in the form of an English parish church, with a castellated tower; the Register list calls it one of the earliest Gothic Revival churches in the country. Washington Irving was a vestryman. The parish also holds a Spanish-language ministry, San Marcos Mission. It was listed on the National Register in 1987, with its later rectory and parish hall.',
  'Red brick with limestone, sandstone and molded brick trim, the front heavy with ivy. Inside, a carved stone altar under a stained-glass window, and Irving\'s pew, one of the original pine furnishings, displayed at the front (Wikipedia).',
  'Washington Irving\'s pew, still in his church.',
  'An active parish at 43 South Broadway (US 9), at Elizabeth Street. Its own website could not be opened when this was written, so service times were not checked; view from the street.',
  [W('Christ Episcopal Church (Tarrytown, New York)')],
  brick='yes',hours='?'),
N('first-baptist-church-tarrytown','First Baptist Church of Tarrytown','Tarrytown','midh','worship','standing',41.07444,-73.85833,'exact','open','drive','','1870s',
  'The congregation first met in the 1840s and built its first church on this lot in 1847. A quarter of a century later Russell Sturgis designed the present stone church in the Victorian Gothic style, which took five years to finish, detailed Gothic interior included. John D. Rockefeller and his family joined the church after moving to the area and paid for later work, among it the landscaping and the rectory. It was listed on the National Register with its rectory in 1983.',
  'A stone Victorian Gothic church at South Broadway and East Elizabeth Street, across from Christ Episcopal Church. Wikipedia describes a detailed Gothic interior; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'An active congregation at 56 South Broadway (US 9). Sunday services at 10:30 a.m. (the church\'s own page, read November 23, 2026); visitors may attend.',
  [W('First Baptist Church of Tarrytown'),L('First Baptist Church of Tarrytown: I\'m new','https://fbctarrytown.org/im-new.html')],
  brick='?',hours='Sunday service 10:30 a.m.'),
N('tarrytown-music-hall','Tarrytown Music Hall','Tarrytown','midh','civic','standing',41.07659,-73.8589,'exact','open','drive','','1885',
  'A Queen Anne theatre of 1885, the oldest in Westchester County still used as one. In 1901 it was one of the first theatres to show motion pictures, and Dave Brubeck, Louis Armstrong and Miles Davis played here. It closed in 1976 from neglect and structural trouble; soon after it was listed on the National Register in 1980 a local non-profit bought and restored it, and it reopened a few years later.',
  'A brick front on a rough granite base with capped towers and an arched brick, stone and terra-cotta panel spelling "Music Hall"; the back is stucco and half-timbering. The inside is seen by going to a show; interior not checked.',
  'An 1885 theatre that Armstrong and Brubeck played, saved from a 1970s closing.',
  'A non-profit theatre at West Main Street and Kaldenberg Place, a block west of Broadway (Wikipedia). Its own website could not be opened when this was written; see its listings for show times. Downtown street parking.',
  [W('Tarrytown Music Hall')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('foster-memorial-ame-zion-tarrytown','Foster Memorial AME Zion Church','Tarrytown','midh','worship','standing',41.07944,-73.86389,'area','view','drive','','1860; church 1865',
  'Formed in 1860, the oldest Black church in Westchester County and possibly one of the oldest in the state. During the Civil War it was a stop on the Underground Railroad, known as the "Freedom Church"; one of its founders and many of its members had escaped slavery themselves, and they helped others go on to Canada or settle in Tarrytown. The brick church, designed by local architect James Bird, opened in 1865 and was listed on the National Register in 1982.',
  'A two-storey church on Wildey Street; its red brick shows only on the front, with granite capstones on pilasters, and the other walls are under an artificial stone veneer (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  'The "Freedom Church" of the Underground Railroad.',
  'An active church on Wildey Street. Service times were not checked; view from the street.',
  [W('Foster Memorial AME Zion Church')],
  brick='some',hours='?'),
N('lyndhurst-tarrytown','Lyndhurst (the Jay Gould estate)','Tarrytown','midh','inst','standing',41.05397,-73.86728,'site','open','drive','','1838–1842',
  'A Gothic Revival country house designed in 1838 by Alexander Jackson Davis and finished in 1842 for New York mayor William Paulding, faced with limestone quarried by prisoners at Sing Sing; critics called it "Paulding\'s Folly". It passed to the merchant George Merritt, who had Davis enlarge it, then to the railroad financier Jay Gould and his daughters Helen and Anna, who changed little. Anna Gould gave the estate to the National Trust for Historic Preservation in 1961, and it was made a National Historic Landmark in 1966.',
  'The turreted stone mansion, kept with its original decorative arts, in a 67-acre park over the Hudson. On the grounds: the 390-foot iron-framed glass conservatory built by Lord & Burnham in 1881 (its restoration is going on in stages), a Shingle-style bowling alley of 1894, a swimming pool building of 1911 and the Rose Cottage playhouse of about 1912 (Wikipedia). The house is seen by guided tour.',
  'A Gothic castle on the Hudson that a robber baron left almost untouched.',
  'Lyndhurst, 635 South Broadway (US 9). Free parking on the estate with a tour ticket or grounds pass; do not park on side streets. Dogs on leashes welcome on the grounds. Grounds open daily April to December, 9:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.; no tours in November; closed January to March except for theatre events, and on Thanksgiving, Christmas Day and New Year\'s Day (Lyndhurst\'s visit page, read November 23, 2026).',
  [W('Lyndhurst (estate)'),L('Lyndhurst: visit','https://lyndhurst.org/visit/')],
  th=[41.05339,-73.86976],thn='Bowling Alley parking',brick='no',hours='Grounds daily April to December 9:30–4:30; house tours 10–4, six days a week most months (7 in October), none in November'),
N('sunnyside-irvington','Sunnyside (Washington Irving\'s house)','Irvington (Tarrytown postal area)','midh','landmark','standing',41.04756,-73.86989,'site','open','drive','','c. 1650 farmhouse; rebuilt from 1835',
  'Washington Irving bought a riverside farm in 1835 for $1,800; at its core was a two-room stone tenant farmhouse of about 1650 on land once part of Philipsburg Manor, owned by the Van Tassel family until 1802. He made it into the picturesque cottage he called Sunnyside, a mix of Dutch, Scottish Gothic and Tudor touches, and died in his bedroom there on November 28, 1859. The Irving family lived in it until 1945, when it was sold to John D. Rockefeller Jr.; it was restored, a Victorian north wing taken down, and opened in 1947. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1962.',
  'The cottage with its wisteria-covered entrance and stepped gables on 10 acres by the river. Guided tours go through the cottage; afterwards visitors may walk the grounds.',
  'The cottage the author of the Headless Horseman built for himself.',
  'Historic Hudson Valley, 3 West Sunnyside Lane, Irvington. Timed tickets, cheaper bought ahead: adults $18 for the standard tour in 2026 (Historic Hudson Valley\'s Sunnyside page, read November 23, 2026).',
  [W('Sunnyside (Tarrytown, New York)'),HHV('Washington Irving\'s Sunnyside','https://hudsonvalley.org/historic-sites/washington-irvings-sunnyside/')],
  brick='?',hours='Friday to Sunday, May 8 to September 13 and November 6 to 29; Saturday and Sunday December 5 to 20 (2026)'),
N('washington-irving-memorial-irvington','Washington Irving Memorial','Irvington','midh','curio','standing',41.04778,-73.86139,'site','view','drive','','1927',
  'A memorial to Washington Irving pushed for from 1909 by a local woman, Jennie Prince Black, at a time when Sunnyside was still a private Irving family home. It took almost twenty years, several sites and many money troubles before it was dedicated in 1927. Daniel Chester French made the bust of Irving and the figures of two of his characters, and Charles A. Platt designed the small stone plaza. It was restored in the late 1900s and listed on the National Register in 2000.',
  'A stone plaza at the street corner with French\'s bronze bust of Irving and his sculptures of two of Irving\'s best-known characters.',
  '',
  'At the corner of Broadway (US 9) and West Sunnyside Lane, beside the road to Sunnyside (Wikipedia). Seen from the sidewalk.',
  [W('Washington Irving Memorial')],
  brick='no'),
N('union-church-pocantico-hills','Union Church of Pocantico Hills','Pocantico Hills, Town of Mount Pleasant','midh','worship','standing',41.09556,-73.83278,'exact','open','drive','','1921',
  'A non-denominational stone church paid for mainly by John D. Rockefeller Jr. in 1921 for the hamlet below the family estate, Kykuit; stone and timber came from the estate. After his wife Abby Aldrich Rockefeller died in 1948 their son Nelson asked Henri Matisse for a rose window in her memory, the artist\'s last commission; after John Jr. died in 1960 his children commissioned Marc Chagall\'s Good Samaritan window. A parish hall was added in 1930–1931, and the church was listed on the National Register in 2006.',
  'A one-storey Gothic-style church of fieldstone under a steep slate roof. Inside, Matisse\'s rose window and nine windows by Chagall (Historic Hudson Valley). Visits start with a short history from a museum educator and last 30 to 45 minutes.',
  'Matisse\'s last commission and nine Chagall windows in a country church.',
  'An active church, 555 Bedford Road (NY 448). Historic Hudson Valley runs visits: $12 timed tickets bought in advance online or at the Philipsburg Manor visitor center, none sold at the door; the schedule can change for church events (Historic Hudson Valley\'s Union Church page, read November 23, 2026).',
  [W('Union Church of Pocantico Hills'),HHV('Union Church of Pocantico Hills','https://hudsonvalley.org/historic-sites/union-church-of-pocantico-hills/')],
  brick='no',hours='Visits May to December: weekends in spring and September, Saturdays in summer and Nov–Dec, Thursday–Sunday in October (2026)'),
N('rockwood-hall-ruins','Rockwood Hall foundation (William Rockefeller\'s mansion)','Sleepy Hollow, Town of Mount Pleasant','midh','inst','ruins',41.11309,-73.86628,'site','public','walk','','1849–1942',
  'Edwin Bartlett built Rockwood, an English Gothic castle of local stone, here about 1849. William Henry Aspinwall and his son owned it from 1860 to 1886; then William Rockefeller, John D. Rockefeller\'s brother and Standard Oil partner, bought it and greatly enlarged it into a mansion of 204 rooms, the second-largest private house in the country at the time. He died there in 1922. Later owners found no use for it, and in late 1941 and early 1942 John D. Rockefeller Jr. had most of the buildings razed, taking care to spare the retaining walls, roads and terraces. The ground is now an 88-acre section of Rockefeller State Park Preserve.',
  'The terraced foundation of the mansion on its bluff over the Hudson, with walled terraces, old carriage roads and specimen trees; the Foundation Loop path goes round it. The gatehouse on US 9 is the other survivor (Wikipedia).',
  'The terraces of a vanished 204-room Rockefeller mansion, open to walk.',
  'Rockwood Hall section of Rockefeller State Park Preserve, New York State Parks. Open all year, sunrise to sunset; picnicking allowed but no fires; no restrooms at Rockwood Hall; pets on a 6-foot leash; deer-management hunting takes place in season (State Parks page, read November 23, 2026). The park page gives no parking directions for the Rockwood Hall section, so parking and any vehicle fee there were not checked; the preserve\'s main entrance has the visitor center and parking.',
  [W('Rockwood Hall'),L('New York State Parks: Rockefeller State Park Preserve','https://parks.ny.gov/parks/rockefeller/details.aspx')],
  brick='no',hours='Every day, sunrise to sunset'),
N('keepers-house-dobbs-ferry','Old Croton Aqueduct Keeper\'s House','Dobbs Ferry','midh','water','standing',41.0115,-73.8768,'site','open','walk','','1857',
  'A brick Italianate house built in 1857 on the Old Croton Aqueduct, home of James Bremner, the aqueduct\'s principal superintendent north of New York City. Keepers\' houses once stood along the line; this is the only one left in its original place. The aqueduct, built 1837–1842, carried Croton River water 41 miles to Manhattan by gravity and served until 1955. The house was restored by 2016 and is now a visitor and education centre.',
  'The brick house on the aqueduct trail at Walnut Street, with exhibits on how the aqueduct was built and who built it, trail maps, water and a restroom. The trail itself, on top of the buried aqueduct, runs north to Croton and south to the Bronx.',
  'The last aqueduct keeper\'s house left where it was built.',
  'On the Old Croton Aqueduct State Historic Park trail at Walnut Street, Dobbs Ferry; the centre is staffed by docents of the Friends of the Old Croton Aqueduct on weekends (the Friends\' page, read November 23, 2026). Street parking nearby; parking not checked.',
  [W('Croton Aqueduct'),L('Friends of the Old Croton Aqueduct: Keeper\'s House','https://aqueduct.org/venue/keepers-house')],
  brick='?',hours='Saturday and Sunday 1–4 p.m.; from December 1, 2026, 12–3 p.m.'),
N('lord-burnham-building-irvington','Lord & Burnham Building (Irvington Public Library)','Irvington','midh','industry','standing',41.03944,-73.8725,'exact','open','walk','','1880s',
  'Lord\'s Horticultural Works, makers of boilers and glass conservatories, moved to Irvington in 1870 to be near the Hudson Valley estates that were its customers. After a fire destroyed its first building it put up this Queen Anne brick works in the 1880s. As Lord & Burnham the firm built greenhouses until 1988; among its work is the conservatory at Lyndhurst. It was listed on the National Register in 1999 and now holds condominiums and the village library.',
  'The brick factory at Main and Astor streets, across Astor Street from the train station, at the bottom of Irvington\'s old Main Street. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'The Irvington Public Library occupies part of the building; the rest is private apartments. Library hours not checked; the station lot and Main Street are close by.',
  [W('Lord & Burnham Building'),L('Irvington Public Library','https://www.irvingtonlibrary.org/')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('draper-observatory-hastings','Henry Draper Observatory (Draper Cottage)','Hastings-on-Hudson','midh','landmark','standing',40.99128,-73.87975,'exact','open','drive','','c. 1860',
  'An astronomical observatory built about 1860 for Henry Draper, who here in 1863 took some of the first photographs of the Moon through a telescope to show recognisable features. He added a second dome; it burned in 1905 and his sister Antonia Draper Dixon rebuilt it. It was made a National Historic Landmark in 1975, on the mistaken belief that it was the house of his father, the scientist John William Draper. The building and Draper Park passed to the village on condition it be a museum, and it is now the home of the Hastings Historical Society.',
  'The cottage-like observatory in Draper Park off US 9, with exhibits of the historical society. Interior not checked beyond the society\'s description.',
  'The cottage where the Moon was first photographed in detail.',
  'Owned by the Village of Hastings-on-Hudson and used by the Hastings Historical Society (Wikipedia; the society\'s page, read November 23, 2026). A small lot off Washington Avenue in Draper Park is mapped on OpenStreetMap.',
  [W('Henry Draper Observatory'),L('Hastings Historical Society','https://www.hastingshistoricalsociety.org/')],
  th=[40.99148,-73.87927],thn='Draper Park lot',brick='?',hours='Monday and Thursday 11 a.m.–2 p.m. all year; first Saturday of the month 2–4 p.m., except July and August'),
N('ever-rest-hastings','Ever Rest (Jasper Cropsey\'s house and studio)','Hastings-on-Hudson','midh','landmark','standing',40.99306,-73.88194,'site','open','drive','','1835; studio 1888',
  'A house built in 1835 that the Hudson River School painter Jasper F. Cropsey bought in 1886; he added a studio, finished in 1888. The Newington-Cropsey Foundation owns and runs it as a house museum with Cropsey\'s work. It was listed on the National Register in 1973.',
  'The house and the artist\'s studio addition; a guided tour of the house takes about 45 minutes. Interior not checked beyond the foundation\'s description.',
  'A Hudson River School painter\'s studio, shown free by appointment.',
  'Newington-Cropsey Foundation. Free guided tours by appointment only, Monday to Thursday at 10, 11 or noon, booked a week ahead by calling (914) 478-7990; groups up to 15. Closed for tours in December, January and August and on federal holidays (the foundation\'s tours page, read November 23, 2026).',
  [W('Ever Rest'),L('Newington-Cropsey Foundation: tours','https://newingtoncropsey.com/tours')],
  brick='?',hours='By appointment, Monday to Thursday 10, 11 or noon; closed December, January and August'),
N('elmsford-reformed-church','Elmsford Reformed Church and Cemetery','Elmsford','midh','worship','standing',41.05389,-73.82028,'exact','view','drive','cemeteries','1793',
  'A two-storey wooden Dutch Reformed meeting house built in 1793 of hand-hewn beams, shingles and hand-wrought nails, for a congregation organised in 1790; it is the oldest building in the village and believed to be the oldest church in the county in regular use. Most of its ornament dates from the 1820s. The small congregation never needed to enlarge or move it. Its 18th-century cemetery holds Revolutionary War veterans, among them Isaac Van Wart, one of the three captors of Major John André, under a tall monument the citizens of Westchester County put up in 1829.',
  'The shingled meeting house at 30 South Central Avenue with its little cemetery dominated by the Van Wart monument. Wikipedia says the exterior is little changed and the interior has seen some changes; interior not checked.',
  'A 1793 meeting house and the grave of one of André\'s captors.',
  'An active church at 30 South Central Avenue (Wikipedia). Service times were not checked; view the church and cemetery from the street.',
  [W('Elmsford Reformed Church and Cemetery')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('philipse-manor-hall-yonkers','Philipse Manor Hall','Yonkers','midh','landmark','standing',40.93556,-73.89972,'site','open','drive','','c. 1682',
  'The oldest part, the southwest corner, was built about 1682 by Frederick Philipse, first lord of Philipsburg Manor, and his wife Margaret Hardenbroeck, a merchant in her own right; their estate came to take in all of Yonkers and much of western Westchester, and the family shipped hundreds of enslaved Africans across the Atlantic. It is the second-oldest building standing in Westchester County. From 1868 to 1908 it was Yonkers\' village and then city hall, and it has been a museum since 1912.',
  'The manor house at Warburton Avenue and Dock Street in Getty Square. Inside, a papier-mâché and plaster Rococo ceiling of 1750, one of two of its kind left in place in the country, and a high vaulted great chamber with woodwork in the manner of an English manor hall (Wikipedia).',
  'A 1680s manor house with a Rococo ceiling, in the middle of Yonkers.',
  'Philipse Manor Hall State Historic Site, New York State Parks, 29 Warburton Avenue. Adults $6, seniors and students $3, children under 12 free; may close for holidays (State Parks page, read November 23, 2026).',
  [W('Philipse Manor Hall State Historic Site'),L('New York State Parks: Philipse Manor Hall','https://parks.ny.gov/historic-sites/philipsemanorhall/details.aspx')],
  brick='?',hours='Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Monday and Tuesday'),
N('untermyer-gardens-yonkers','Untermyer Park and Gardens','Yonkers','midh','inst','standing',40.96725,-73.88642,'site','open','walk','','1916 on',
  'What is left of Greystone, the 150-acre estate of the lawyer Samuel Untermyer, who from 1916 had William W. Bosworth lay out gardens that were among the finest in the country and often open to the public. After Untermyer died in 1940 Yonkers took part of the estate as a park, which decayed through the 1970s, with vandalism and a short-lived restoration. Since 2011 the Untermyer Gardens Conservancy has been restoring it with the city parks department. It was listed on the National Register in 1974.',
  'The Walled Garden in the Indo-Persian manner, with a small open-air amphitheatre under sphinxes on Ionic columns, a classical pavilion, a stoa and the round Temple of the Sky; a long stair down to an overlook of the river and the Palisades. The estate\'s old gatehouse is now the Ruin Garden (2019), left roofless with its graffiti kept and planted to feel like an abandoned house (Wikipedia).',
  'A Gilded Age garden that fell to ruin and is being brought back, gatehouse ruin and all.',
  'City of Yonkers park, restored with the Untermyer Gardens Conservancy, 945 North Broadway; lower entrance at 917 Warburton Avenue. About 75 parking spaces plus street parking on North Broadway; no buses in the lot. Dogs are not allowed in the garden. Groups of 20 or more need a city permit (914-377-6450). Closed Thanksgiving and New Year\'s Day among other days (the conservancy\'s visit page, read November 23, 2026).',
  [W('Untermyer Park and Gardens'),L('Untermyer Gardens Conservancy: visit','https://www.untermyergardens.org/visit')],
  brick='?',hours='Daily from 9 a.m.; closes 4:30 p.m. Nov 2–Mar 8, 6 p.m. Mar 9–Apr 30 and Sep 16–Nov 1, 7 p.m. May 1–Sep 15'),
]
